WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.153
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book four.

2
00:00:05.453 --> 00:00:09.013
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.313 --> 00:00:15.634
Unit twelve: Competitive Strategy. Today you will compare strategic options with either / or structures.

4
00:00:15.934 --> 00:00:18.506
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.467 --> 00:00:22.421
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.283 --> 00:00:24.958
Competitive strategy.

7
00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:30.771
Competitive strategy means a long-term plan to gain advantage over rivals.

8
00:00:31.383 --> 00:00:34.521
Their competitive strategy relies on speed of delivery.

9
00:00:35.117 --> 00:00:38.582
Your turn. Say: Competitive strategy.

10
00:00:41.433 --> 00:00:43.050
Competitive strategy.

11
00:00:43.783 --> 00:00:45.075
Cost leadership.

12
00:00:46.083 --> 00:00:50.393
Cost leadership means competing by being the lowest-cost producer.

13
00:00:50.983 --> 00:00:54.666
Cost leadership requires huge volumes and strict efficiency.

14
00:00:55.250 --> 00:00:58.518
Your turn. Say: Cost leadership.

15
00:01:01.367 --> 00:01:02.890
Cost leadership.

16
00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:05.278
Differentiation strategy.

17
00:01:06.283 --> 00:01:11.360
Differentiation strategy means competing by offering unique features customers will pay more for.

18
00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:15.714
Their differentiation strategy focuses on organic ingredients.

19
00:01:16.317 --> 00:01:19.654
Your turn. Say: Differentiation strategy.

20
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:24.279
Differentiation strategy.

21
00:01:25.017 --> 00:01:26.122
Niche market.

22
00:01:27.117 --> 00:01:30.765
Niche market means a small, specialised segment of a larger market.

23
00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:34.000
Vegan Khmer cuisine is a growing niche market.

24
00:01:34.583 --> 00:01:37.468
Your turn. Say: Niche market.

25
00:01:40.333 --> 00:01:41.369
Niche market.

26
00:01:42.117 --> 00:01:43.559
Barriers to entry.

27
00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:48.790
Barriers to entry means obstacles that prevent new competitors from entering.

28
00:01:49.383 --> 00:01:52.138
Patents create barriers to entry for rivals.

29
00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:55.646
Your turn. Say: Barriers to entry.

30
00:01:58.500 --> 00:01:59.954
Barriers to entry.

31
00:02:00.717 --> 00:02:02.263
First-mover advantage.

32
00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:07.386
First-mover advantage means the benefit of being the first company in a market.

33
00:02:07.983 --> 00:02:11.608
Their first-mover advantage faded once larger firms arrived.

34
00:02:12.217 --> 00:02:15.391
Your turn. Say: First-mover advantage.

35
00:02:18.233 --> 00:02:20.175
First-mover advantage.

36
00:02:20.917 --> 00:02:21.918
Benchmarking.

37
00:02:22.917 --> 00:02:26.356
Benchmarking means comparing performance with the best in the industry.

38
00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:30.122
Benchmarking against Thai hotels improved their service.

39
00:02:30.717 --> 00:02:33.392
Your turn. Say: Benchmarking.

40
00:02:36.233 --> 00:02:37.292
Benchmarking.

41
00:02:38.050 --> 00:02:39.690
Sustainable advantage.

42
00:02:40.683 --> 00:02:45.701
Sustainable advantage means an advantage that rivals cannot easily copy over time.

43
00:02:46.317 --> 00:02:49.791
Their sustainable advantage is a network of loyal farmers.

44
00:02:50.383 --> 00:02:53.639
Your turn. Say: Sustainable advantage.

45
00:02:56.500 --> 00:02:58.209
Sustainable advantage.

46
00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:02.372
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:03:03.217 --> 00:03:09.294
Visal, a Kampot pepper producer can't match the prices of mass-market pepper. Should it cut costs?

48
00:03:10.383 --> 00:03:21.732
It can compete either on cost or on differentiation, but rarely on both. With small farms, cost leadership is unrealistic.

49
00:03:22.817 --> 00:03:24.851
So it should focus on quality?

50
00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:36.323
Exactly. Kampot pepper has Geographical Indication status, so it can target a premium niche market where buyers pay for origin and taste.

51
00:03:37.417 --> 00:03:39.892
What if a large company copies the idea?

52
00:03:40.983 --> 00:03:51.182
The GI label is a barrier to entry. Neither cheaper rivals nor big brands can legally call their pepper 'Kampot', which makes the advantage sustainable.

53
00:03:52.333 --> 00:03:54.159
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:55.017 --> 00:03:58.026
Grammar focus: Either and or structures.

55
00:03:58.326 --> 00:03:59.467
Look at the pattern.

56
00:04:00.183 --> 00:04:03.704
A company can compete either on cost or on differentiation.

57
00:04:04.617 --> 00:04:08.067
The firm must either lower its prices or improve its quality.

58
00:04:08.983 --> 00:04:12.527
Neither cheaper rivals nor big brands can use the GI label.

59
00:04:13.467 --> 00:04:22.516
Keep the structure parallel: either + noun … or + noun; either + verb … or + verb. With 'neither … nor', the verb agrees with the nearer subject.

60
00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:27.516
Be careful! Don't say: Either we cut costs or differentiate.

61
00:04:29.467 --> 00:04:32.023
Say: We either cut costs or differentiate.

62
00:04:32.323 --> 00:04:34.323
Place 'either' before the first option.

63
00:04:35.133 --> 00:04:37.937
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:38.733 --> 00:04:39.862
Stuck in the Middle.

65
00:04:40.162 --> 00:04:47.769
Michael Porter argued that firms achieve superior performance either by becoming the lowest-cost producer or by offering something distinctive.

66
00:04:48.767 --> 00:04:56.548
Companies that try to do both often end up 'stuck in the middle', neither cheap enough to win on price nor special enough to justify a premium.

67
00:04:57.533 --> 00:05:03.980
Smaller firms frequently choose a focus strategy, serving a niche market more effectively than larger rivals can.

68
00:05:04.967 --> 00:05:12.852
Advantages last only when they are protected by barriers to entry, such as patents, brand reputation or exclusive relationships.

69
00:05:13.867 --> 00:05:20.708
Kampot pepper shows how a protected geographical name can turn a small producer's origin into a sustainable advantage.

70
00:05:21.800 --> 00:05:25.732
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:26.583 --> 00:05:29.082
You can pay blank by cash or by card.

72
00:05:29.382 --> 00:05:32.467
A, both. B, neither. C, either.

73
00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:38.046
The answer is C: either.

74
00:05:39.183 --> 00:05:42.332
Competing by being the lowest-cost producer is blank.

75
00:05:42.632 --> 00:05:47.133
A, cost leadership. B, benchmarking. C, a niche market.

76
00:05:50.667 --> 00:05:53.328
The answer is A: cost leadership.

77
00:05:54.483 --> 00:05:57.725
Comparing performance with the best in the industry is blank.

78
00:05:58.025 --> 00:06:03.327
A, first-mover advantage. B, benchmarking. C, differentiation.

79
00:06:06.867 --> 00:06:09.029
The answer is B: benchmarking.

80
00:06:10.183 --> 00:06:12.044
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:06:12.344 --> 00:06:20.571
A, Neither the price nor the quality were good.. B, Neither the price or the quality was good.. C, Neither the price nor the quality was good..

82
00:06:24.133 --> 00:06:27.909
The answer is C: Neither the price nor the quality was good..

83
00:06:29.050 --> 00:06:32.257
According to the reading, firms 'stuck in the middle' are…

84
00:06:32.557 --> 00:06:38.405
A, neither cheap enough nor special enough. B, the most profitable. C, always small.

85
00:06:41.967 --> 00:06:45.092
The answer is A: neither cheap enough nor special enough.

86
00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:49.254
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:50.117 --> 00:06:53.779
Would you rather compete on cost or on differentiation? Why?

88
00:07:10.583 --> 00:07:12.153
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:07:12.453 --> 00:07:46.609
I would rather compete on differentiation, because cost leadership usually requires enormous scale, which most new businesses cannot achieve. A firm that offers something distinctive, such as excellent design, local ingredients or outstanding service, can charge higher prices and build loyal customers. A small Cambodian silk workshop could never win on price, but it can sell authentic handwoven products to buyers who value craftsmanship. Differentiation is harder to copy, so it lasts longer.

90
00:07:46.909 --> 00:07:48.653
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:49.550 --> 00:07:54.072
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and either and or structures.

92
00:07:54.372 --> 00:07:59.485
Today's value is strategic thinking. Think ahead, and choose your path with purpose.

93
00:07:59.785 --> 00:08:02.542
See you in Unit thirteen. Goodbye!

94
00:08:02.842 --> 00:08:05.355
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

